# The last page (plan/16 §5.3, replaced in plan/28 §6).
#
# It is `done` on disk and "Finish" in the sidebar: a plugin called `finished` would collide
# file-for-file with app-admin/calamares' own module. The two keys below are UPSTREAM'S, by
# upstream's names and with upstream's aliases, because the page changed hands and the
# configuration contract did not.
#
# `notifyOnFinished` is gone with the module that read it. It was false here and always had been —
# a D-Bus desktop notification about an installer that is the only application on the screen has
# no second window to be seen from — so the replacement does not implement it at all, which drops
# a QtDBus dependency and a code path nothing here exercised.
---
# The reboot checkbox is offered, unchecked. Unchecked deliberately: the user has to REMOVE THE
# INSTALL MEDIUM before rebooting, and a pre-ticked box invites them not to. The page says so, in
# a line that appears only once the box IS ticked.
#
# THE LABEL CLASH plan/33 §2 REMOVED IS NOT WHY THIS REMINDER STAYS. Until that document a live
# medium's own three partitions carried the SAME PARTLABELs an installed disk's do — root_@VERSION@
# and var — because those strings are the installed system's identity and are deliberately not
# profile-suffixed (plan/16 §3.4). With both disks attached, /dev/disk/by-partlabel/ resolved each
# name to whichever device udev saw first, so booting with the stick still in could mix the live
# root with the installed /var — a real, per-partition ambiguity, and the reason for the line
# below existed in the first place. A medium built after plan/33 §2 carries live_esp/
# live_root_@VERSION@/live_var instead, so that specific mix can no longer happen.
#
# The reminder is kept anyway, for a plainer reason: this machine's firmware may still have the
# stick's EFI boot entry ahead of the disk's in its own menu, or configured as the default. That
# has nothing to do with partition labels — it boots the WRONG THING, not the right thing wired
# to the wrong data — and removing the medium is still the simplest way to make sure firmware
# does not do it.
restartNowMode:     user-unchecked
restartNowCommand:  "systemctl -i reboot"
